\paragraph{Temporal embedding.}
A learned embedding $e(\dt)$ is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step to condition state predictions on the temporal scale. We test three modes:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Trained}: embedding parameters are updated via backpropagation through the world model loss, so the RSSM can co-adapt its dynamics and reward predictions to the temporal scale.
\item \textbf{Frozen}: a fixed random projection of identical dimensionality (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}). The RSSM receives a temporal signal but cannot co-adapt to it.
\item \textbf{Removed}: embedding dimension set to zero; no temporal conditioning signal is provided.
\end{itemize}
We further distinguish \textbf{trained-and-exposed} (subsample ON: the RSSM trains on $\dt{>}1$ transitions) from \textbf{trained-but-unexposed} (subsample OFF: $\dt{>}1$ used only during imagination). This four-way classification (trained/exposed, trained/unexposed, frozen, removed) is the key experimental structure of our factorial design.

\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}
When enabled, the replay buffer augments training data with variable-$\dt$ transitions by subsampling consecutive frames. Concretely, with a maximum subsampling factor of~2, the RSSM trains on both $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ transitions drawn from the same episodes. When disabled, only standard $\dt{=}1$ transitions are used. This factor controls whether the world model has seen multi-scale dynamics during training.

\paragraph{Imagination horizon and step size.}
The imagination horizon $H$ sets the number of rollout steps. We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$. The reduced horizon controls for compounding error: $H{=}8$ steps at $\dt{=}2$ covers $16$ physical time steps, comparable to the $15$ physical steps of the default configuration. The parameter \texttt{gate\_fixed\_dt} forces all imagination steps to use a specific $\dt$ value ($1$, $2$, or $4$); this separates the effect of $\dt$-conditioning from any learned gating policy.

\subsection{Factorial Design}
\label{sec:factorial}

We adopt a factorial ablation across four factors: $\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\dt{=}1$, $2$, $4$). The design (12 conditions, Table~\ref{tab:factorial_design} in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}) isolates trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as orthogonal factors. Key configurations use 4--5 seeds (Walker Walk uses 5 seeds to strengthen statistical power; other environments use 2--4 seeds); following \citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories.

\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$).}
We define $\nve_t = (v/e)_{\text{config},t} / (v/e)_{\text{baseline},t}$, where $v/e$ is the critic's mean imagined value divided by mean evaluation return, and the baseline is the highest-performing seed (exp\_001, seed~1, 505K steps). We restrict to a \textbf{35K+ window} to avoid early-training inflation. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold achieves 27/30 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.957$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated, mean AUROC${=}0.988$, Appendix~Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}).
\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping}
\label{sec:sgs_method}

To identify which gradient pathway through $e_{\Delta t}$ drives CIVO, we introduce \textit{selective gradient stopping} (SGS): $e_{\Delta t}$ is detached via $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$ before selected prediction heads during the backward pass, preventing those loss gradients from updating $e_{\Delta t}$ while preserving its forward-pass contribution. We test three variants: reward-only (exp\_016e), full-pathway (exp\_018, all non-KL gradients blocked), and obs-decoder-only (exp\_025/026). Gradient decomposition using $\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_i$ quantifies each pathway's contribution (Appendix~\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}).

\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_pass_method}

We test two forward-pass interventions as representation- and output-level probes: \textbf{TCN} adds Gaussian noise $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ to $e_{\Delta t}$ during imagination ($\sigma \in \{0.5, 0.2\}$, three environments); \textbf{RAVE} clips imagined values exceeding an EMA anchor ($c \in \{2.0, 1.5\}$).

\subsection{Dual-Critic Mechanism Ablation}
\label{sec:dual_critic_method}

To test whether standard overestimation countermeasures address CIVO, we conduct a $2{\times}2$ factorial: \{single, dual\} $\times$ \{narrow (${\sim}1.2$M), wide (${\sim}2.4$M)\} critic parameters. The dual-critic variant follows TD3 \citep{fujimoto2018td3}: two independent value heads trained on shared RSSM features, with the actor optimizing $\min(v_1, v_2)$. The wide variant doubles hidden dimensions to control for capacity independently of the min operation.

\subsection{Environment and Training Details}
\label{sec:env_details}

All experiments use Walker Walk and Cheetah Run from the DeepMind Control Suite (DMC; \citealt{tassa2018dmcontrol}) with proprioceptive observations, chosen for their contrasting dynamics (6-joint biped vs.\ 2-joint runner). Walker Walk serves as the primary environment with the full factorial design; Cheetah Run serves as a severity-dependent generalization probe: CIVO manifests at $8$--$70\times$ NVE on Cheetah versus $2$--$5\times$ on Walker, testing whether interventions transfer under amplified severity rather than replicating the full factorial. The baseline runs for 500K steps (peak return 970.4); treatments run for 105K steps, sufficient to observe collapse onset. All hyperparameters follow the DreamerV3 defaults \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} without task-specific tuning ($d{=}32$ temporal embedding; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{sec:hyperparameters}). Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (${\sim}80$ GPU-hours total). The codebase extends the official DreamerV3 implementation\footnote{\url{https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3}, accessed March 2026.} with the variable-rate modules described in \S\ref{sec:variable_rate}.

